$DIR/main.p:34:10: cannot pass elem(&arr) to call, which keeps it after returning
    elem(&arr) points into arr. arr is a local array of f (declared at
    main.p:33). call passes s to s.String (which may call main.(*S).String)
    (main.p:23), which stores s in package variable keepS (main.p:13), so
    elem(&arr) would outlive arr.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If call should keep its own copy of arr, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[[2]S] from new, a shared[[2]S],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
